    // Part 4 — ma'ruza: marjon riflari
    private static ListeningPart Listening3Part4() => new(4,
        "You will hear part of a lecture about coral reefs.",
        [
            F("Lecturer", "Good morning, everyone. Today's lecture is about coral reefs: what they are, why they matter, and what is being done to protect them."),
            F("Lecturer", "Let's start with a question that surprises many people. Is coral a plant, a rock or an animal? The answer is that corals are animals. Each coral is made up of many tiny individuals called polyps, and they are actually related to jellyfish. A single polyp may be only a few millimetres across, but together, over thousands of years, they build enormous structures."),
            F("Lecturer", "The secret of their success is a partnership. Living inside the tissues of most reef-building corals are microscopic algae. The algae use sunlight to carry out photosynthesis, and they pass on sugars to the coral, which provide most of its energy. In return, the coral gives the algae a safe home and the nutrients they need. The algae are also responsible for the beautiful colour of healthy coral. Coral tissue itself is largely transparent."),
            F("Lecturer", "Because the algae depend on sunlight, reef-building corals can only grow in water that is shallow and clear. They also need warm water, which is why most reefs are found in the tropics."),
            F("Lecturer", "So how is the reef itself built? Each polyp produces a hard skeleton beneath its body, made of limestone, which it takes from the minerals in seawater. When the polyps die, their skeletons remain, and new polyps grow on top of them. Over long periods, this builds up into a reef. Australia's Great Barrier Reef, the largest in the world, is made up of thousands of individual reefs and stretches for more than two thousand kilometres."),
            F("Lecturer", "Why do reefs matter? Well, although they cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, it's estimated that they provide a home for about a quarter of all marine species. They are also important for people. Many coastal communities depend on reef fish for food. And reefs act as natural barriers: they reduce the energy of waves before they reach the shore, which protects coastlines from erosion and storm damage."),
            F("Lecturer", "Unfortunately, reefs face serious threats. The most widespread is coral bleaching. When water temperatures rise above normal for several weeks, the relationship between the coral and its algae breaks down, and the coral pushes the algae out of its tissues. Without the algae, the coral loses its colour and appears white. Bleached coral is not dead, and it can recover if temperatures return to normal quickly, but if the heat continues, the coral may starve."),
            F("Lecturer", "Other threats include pollution from farmland, damage from boats and anchors, and overfishing. And as the oceans absorb more carbon dioxide, the water becomes more acidic, which makes it harder for corals to build their skeletons."),
            F("Lecturer", "Is there any good news? Yes. Scientists and local communities around the world are working to restore damaged reefs. One method is sometimes called coral gardening. Small fragments of healthy coral are collected and grown in underwater nurseries, often hanging from ropes or metal frames. When they are large enough, they are planted back onto the damaged reef."),
            F("Lecturer", "Researchers are also studying corals that survive in unusually warm waters, and trying to breed corals that can tolerate heat better than others. This work is still at an early stage, and scientists agree that it cannot replace action to reduce global warming. But it may help to buy reefs some time. Next week, we'll look at one reef restoration project in detail."),
        ],
        [
            new("gap", NotesOneWord, 1,
            [
                Gap(31, "Corals are animals which are related to ___", ["jellyfish"], "\"They are actually related to jellyfish.\""),
                Gap(32, "Algae inside the coral provide it with ___ for energy", ["sugars", "sugar"], "\"They pass on sugars to the coral, which provide most of its energy.\""),
                Gap(33, "The algae also give healthy coral its ___", ["colour", "color"], "\"The algae are also responsible for the beautiful colour of healthy coral.\""),
                Gap(34, "Reef-building corals need shallow, clear water because the algae need ___", ["sunlight", "light"], "\"Because the algae depend on sunlight, reef-building corals can only grow in water that is shallow and clear.\""),
                Gap(35, "Each polyp builds a hard skeleton made of ___", ["limestone"], "\"Each polyp produces a hard skeleton beneath its body, made of limestone.\""),
                Gap(36, "Reefs are home to about a ___ of all marine species", ["quarter"], "\"They provide a home for about a quarter of all marine species.\""),
                Gap(37, "Reefs protect coastlines by reducing the energy of ___", ["waves"], "\"They reduce the energy of waves before they reach the shore.\""),
                Gap(38, "Bleaching happens when water ___ stay above normal", ["temperatures"], "\"When water temperatures rise above normal for several weeks.\""),
                Gap(39, "Coral gardening: fragments are grown in underwater ___", ["nurseries"], "\"Grown in underwater nurseries, often hanging from ropes or metal frames.\""),
                Gap(40, "Scientists are trying to breed corals that can tolerate ___", ["heat"], "\"Trying to breed corals that can tolerate heat better than others.\""),
            ]),
        ]);

