    // Part 3 — biznes talabalari: kichik novvoyxona keysi
    private static ListeningPart Listening4Part3() => new(3,
        "You will hear two business students, Ruth and Karim, discussing a case study about a small family bakery.",
        [
            M("Karim", "So, Ruth, have you finished reading the case study about Hartley's Bakery?"),
            F("Ruth", "Yes, twice. It's an interesting one. So, the bakery had been doing well for about thirty years, and then everything changed quite suddenly."),
            M("Karim", "Right. I thought at first the problem was the rent going up, because that's mentioned on the first page."),
            F("Ruth", "It is mentioned, but the rent only went up slightly. The real problem was the large supermarket that opened on the same street, with its own in-store bakery. Sales at Hartley's fell by almost a third in six months."),
            M("Karim", "True. I think it's a really useful case, actually, because there's so much data in it."),
            F("Ruth", "Do you think so? I found there wasn't much financial information at all. For me, the value of it is that it shows a small business can survive against a much bigger competitor if it concentrates on quality rather than price."),
            M("Karim", "Hmm, fair enough. And that's interesting, because the owner, Mrs Hartley, didn't do that at first, did she? Her first reaction was to cut her prices."),
            F("Ruth", "Yes, by about fifteen per cent. And it didn't work."),
            M("Karim", "I thought it failed because customers didn't notice the lower prices."),
            F("Ruth", "Some of them probably didn't, but the main issue was that her profit margins were already very small. At the lower prices, she was losing money on some products, so she couldn't keep it up for long."),
            M("Karim", "And the supermarket could always go lower anyway. So then what did she do? She didn't bring in a consultant, did she?"),
            F("Ruth", "No, she couldn't afford one. She asked her customers. She put a short questionnaire on the counter and asked them what they wanted from a local bakery. That's what gave her most of her ideas."),
            M("Karim", "OK, so let's go through the changes she made, because we need to evaluate each one. First, opening on Sundays."),
            F("Ruth", "That brought in people who'd never been in before, especially families going to the park. So in terms of attracting new customers, that was a real success."),
            M("Karim", "Agreed. Then there was the social media campaign. I think that was a mistake, to be honest. She paid an agency a lot of money, and the posts were very professional, but there's no evidence that sales went up."),
            F("Ruth", "Yes, it cost far too much for a business that size. What about selling yesterday's bread at half price in the morning?"),
            M("Karim", "I'm not sure it brought in many new customers. But the case study says the amount she threw away each week fell by more than half."),
            F("Ruth", "So its main effect was less waste. Then there's the wholesale idea, supplying bread to local cafés."),
            M("Karim", "That's the one that worries me. She agreed to supply six cafés all at once, and she couldn't keep up. Some deliveries were late."),
