    // Part 4 — ma'ruza: kitob bosishning ixtiro qilinishi
    private static ListeningPart Listening4Part4() => new(4,
        "You will hear part of a lecture about the invention of printing.",
        [
            M("Lecturer", "Good morning. Today I want to talk about the invention of printing, which many historians consider one of the most important developments in the history of communication. When people think of printing, they usually think of Gutenberg in Germany. But the story begins much earlier, and much further east."),
            M("Lecturer", "The earliest printing we know of was done in East Asia, using a method called woodblock printing. A whole page of text was carved in reverse into a flat piece of wood. The block was covered with ink, and paper was pressed onto it. The oldest printed book with a date on it is a Buddhist text from China, printed in the year 868 using carved wooden blocks. It is a scroll more than five metres long."),
            M("Lecturer", "Woodblocks worked well, but each block could only print one page. The next step was movable type: individual characters that could be arranged, used, and then rearranged for another page. In the eleventh century, a Chinese craftsman called Bi Sheng made movable type from baked clay. However, the technique did not replace woodblocks in China. One reason is that written Chinese uses thousands of characters, so a printer needed an enormous number of pieces of type, and finding the right one took a long time."),
            M("Lecturer", "In Korea, printers went a step further and used type made of metal, which was much more durable than clay. The oldest surviving book printed with metal type was produced in Korea in 1377, more than seventy years before Gutenberg's famous Bible."),
            M("Lecturer", "Now let's turn to Europe. Johannes Gutenberg began his experiments in the city of Mainz in the 1440s. It's important to understand that Gutenberg did not invent any one single thing. His achievement was to combine several technologies into a system that worked. The first was a hand mould, which allowed a worker to cast large numbers of identical metal letters quickly and cheaply. The European alphabet, with only a few dozen letters and symbols, was well suited to this."),
            M("Lecturer", "The second was the ink. The water-based inks used by scribes simply ran off metal type, so Gutenberg developed an ink based on oil, which was thick enough to stick to the letters. And third, there was the press itself. Gutenberg adapted a screw press of the kind farmers had used for centuries to make wine, which could apply strong, even pressure across the whole page."),
            M("Lecturer", "Gutenberg's Bible was completed around 1455. About a hundred and eighty copies were printed, and fewer than fifty survive today. After that, the technology spread remarkably fast. By 1500, printing presses were working in well over two hundred towns across Europe."),
            M("Lecturer", "Books printed in Europe before 1501 are known by a special name, incunabula, which comes from a Latin word meaning 'cradle', because these were the books from the cradle, or earliest days, of printing."),
            M("Lecturer", "What were the effects? First, books became much cheaper, and far more people could own them. Second, printing gradually encouraged more standard spelling. When a scribe copied by hand, he often spelt words in his own way, but printers wanted consistency across thousands of copies. And third, printing had a huge impact on science. When drawings were copied by hand, errors crept in every time. With printing, scientists in different countries could study exactly the same diagrams, whether of plants, human anatomy or machines, and discuss them with confidence."),
            M("Lecturer", "Next week we'll look at how printing changed the way people read, and the rise of newspapers."),
        ],
        [
            new("gap", NotesOneWord, 1,
            [
                Gap(31, "Earliest dated printed book (868): printed from carved wooden ___", ["blocks"], "\"Printed in the year 868 using carved wooden blocks.\""),
                Gap(32, "Bi Sheng (11th century): made movable type from baked ___", ["clay"], "\"Bi Sheng made movable type from baked clay.\""),
                Gap(33, "Problem in China: written Chinese uses thousands of ___", ["characters"], "\"Written Chinese uses thousands of characters.\""),
                Gap(34, "Korea: printers used type made of ___", ["metal"], "\"In Korea, printers went a step further and used type made of metal.\""),
                Gap(35, "Gutenberg: a hand ___ made it possible to cast many identical letters", ["mould", "mold"], "\"A hand mould, which allowed a worker to cast large numbers of identical metal letters.\""),
                Gap(36, "Gutenberg's ink was based on ___", ["oil"], "\"Gutenberg developed an ink based on oil.\""),
                Gap(37, "His press was adapted from a press used to make ___", ["wine"], "\"A screw press of the kind farmers had used for centuries to make wine.\""),
                Gap(38, "'Incunabula' comes from a Latin word meaning ___", ["cradle"], "\"A Latin word meaning 'cradle'.\""),
                Gap(39, "Effects: printing encouraged more standard ___", ["spelling"], "\"Printing gradually encouraged more standard spelling.\""),
                Gap(40, "Scientists in different countries could study exactly the same ___", ["diagrams"], "\"Scientists in different countries could study exactly the same diagrams.\""),
            ]),
        ]);

