    // Part 4 — ma'ruza: sahro me'morchiligida shamol minoralari
    private static ListeningPart Listening5Part4() => new(4,
        "You will hear part of a lecture about traditional wind towers and cooling in desert architecture.",
        [
            F("Lecturer", "Good afternoon. In this series we've been looking at how buildings can be kept comfortable without using large amounts of energy. Today, I'm going to talk about some of the oldest and most elegant solutions: the traditional wind towers and other cooling techniques developed in the hot, dry regions of the Middle East."),
            F("Lecturer", "Wind towers, sometimes called wind catchers, have been used for many centuries in Iran and around the Persian Gulf. The Iranian city of Yazd is famous for them. From a distance, you can see dozens of tall, rectangular towers rising above the flat roofs of the houses."),
            F("Lecturer", "The basic idea is simple. Near the ground, the air in a desert town moves slowly and carries a lot of sand. But a few metres higher up, the wind is stronger and contains less dust. The tower catches this cleaner, faster-moving air through openings near the top and directs it down a vertical shaft into the rooms below."),
            F("Lecturer", "Builders were careful about which way the openings faced. In some areas, towers were open on only one side, facing the direction of the prevailing wind, which is the direction from which the wind usually blows. In other places, towers had openings on all four sides, so they could catch the wind whatever direction it came from."),
            F("Lecturer", "Inside the tower, the shaft is usually divided by internal partitions. These allow fresh air to travel down one channel while warm air from the house travels up and out through another."),
            F("Lecturer", "And what happens when there's no wind at all? Here the tower works in a different way. In the afternoon, the sun heats the tower, and the warm air inside it rises and escapes at the top. This draws air up from the rooms below, so the tower acts like a chimney, keeping the air in the house moving even on a still day."),
            F("Lecturer", "In some buildings, the cooling effect was increased by water. Many desert towns were supplied by a qanat, an underground channel which carried water from the hills, sometimes over many kilometres. In certain houses, air from the wind tower passed over a pool fed by the qanat before entering the living space. As some of the water evaporated, it took heat from the air. This cooling by evaporation could lower the temperature of the air considerably."),
            F("Lecturer", "Wind towers were only one part of a complete system. Walls were built of mud brick and were often very thick. During the day, they absorbed heat slowly, so the rooms inside stayed cool. Then, at night, when the desert air became cold, the walls released that stored heat, keeping the rooms comfortable."),
            F("Lecturer", "Houses were also usually built around a central courtyard, often with a small pool and a few trees. The courtyard provided light and fresh air to the rooms around it, while its high walls kept much of it out of direct sunlight. And at the scale of the whole town, streets were narrow and winding, so the buildings on each side kept them in shade for most of the day."),
            F("Lecturer", "Why does this matter today? Modern air-conditioning systems consume huge amounts of energy. In recent years, several architects have included modern versions of the wind tower in new buildings in hot countries, sometimes combined with sensors and fans. Studies suggest that these designs can significantly reduce the demand for electricity. So, a technology that is centuries old may help us build more sustainable cities in the future."),
        ],
        [
            new("gap", NotesOneWord, 1,
            [
                Gap(31, "Higher up, the wind is stronger and contains less ___", ["dust"], "\"A few metres higher up, the wind is stronger and contains less dust.\""),
                Gap(32, "Some towers faced the direction of the ___ wind", ["prevailing"], "\"Facing the direction of the prevailing wind.\""),
                Gap(33, "Inside, the shaft is divided by ___", ["partitions"], "\"The shaft is usually divided by internal partitions.\""),
                Gap(34, "With no wind, the tower works like a ___", ["chimney"], "\"The tower acts like a chimney.\""),
                Gap(35, "Water came from a ___, an underground channel", ["qanat"], "\"Many desert towns were supplied by a qanat, an underground channel.\""),
                Gap(36, "Air was cooled by ___ as it passed over water", ["evaporation"], "\"This cooling by evaporation could lower the temperature of the air considerably.\""),
                Gap(37, "Thick mud-brick walls release stored heat at ___", ["night"], "\"Then, at night ... the walls released that stored heat.\""),
                Gap(38, "Houses were built around a central ___", ["courtyard"], "\"Houses were also usually built around a central courtyard.\""),
                Gap(39, "Narrow streets stayed in ___ for most of the day", ["shade"], "\"The buildings on each side kept them in shade for most of the day.\""),
                Gap(40, "Modern wind towers can reduce the demand for ___", ["electricity"], "\"These designs can significantly reduce the demand for electricity.\""),
            ]),
        ]);

